+\subsection{Twist-Rotor Status and Micro--Macro Participation Audit}
+\label{subsec:rt_twist_rotor_participation_audit}
+
+\paragraph{Scope and status.}
+\textbf{[RESEARCH TRACK / AUDIT GUARD].}
+This subsection adjudicates the compact-rotor proposal without promoting a
+new SST constant.  It distinguishes a useful twist-channel interpretation
+from a numerical derivation and makes the missing micro--macro participation
+factor explicit.
+
+\paragraph{Compact-rotor matching is a reformulation.}
+For a compact angular rotor with spectrum
+\begin{align}
+    E_n
+    =
+    \frac{n^2\hbar^2}{2I_\ell},
+\end{align}
+the matching postulate
+\begin{align}
+    \Delta E_{01}
+    =
+    \frac{\hbar^2}{2I_\ell^{\rm match}}
+    \stackrel{\rm ansatz}{=}
+    \hbar\omega_c
+    =
+    m_ec^2
+\end{align}
+gives
+\begin{align}
+    I_\ell^{\rm match}
+    =
+    \frac{\hbar}{2\omega_c},
+    \qquad
+    \mathcal J_{\omega}^{\rm rot}
+    :=
+    \frac{I_\ell^{\rm match}}{\rc^3}
+    =
+    \frac{\hbar}{2\omega_c\rc^3}.
+    \label{eq:rt_rotor_matching_microinertia}
+\end{align}
+Using $\omega_c=\vchar/\rc$, the horn-envelope closure
+Eq.~\eqref{eq:core_density}, and the calibrated electron chain, this becomes
+\begin{align}
+    \boxed{
+    \mathcal J_{\omega}^{\rm rot}
+    =
+    \pi\rc^2\rhohorn
+    =:
+    \mu_{\ell}^{\rm horn}
+    }
+    =
+    2.4282114\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-1}}.
+    \label{eq:rt_rotor_line_inertia_reformulation}
+\end{align}
+\textbf{[REFORMULATION / MATCHING ANSATZ].}
+The numerical value is inherited from the same Compton--horn calibration
+chain.  Equation~\eqref{eq:rt_rotor_line_inertia_reformulation} therefore
+interprets the horn-envelope line-inertia coefficient as twist-channel
+microinertia; it does not derive an independent constant and does not identify
+$\mu_{\ell}^{\rm horn}$ with a resolved material filament mass.
+
+\paragraph{The wave-speed result is definitional.}
+If one further defines
+\begin{align}
+    K_\ell
+    :=
+    I_\ell^{\rm match}\omega_c^2,
+    \qquad
+    \mathcal K_\omega
+    :=
+    \frac{K_\ell}{\rc},
+\end{align}
+then
+\begin{align}
+    c_\omega^2
+    :=
+    \frac{\mathcal K_\omega}{\mathcal J_{\omega}^{\rm rot}}
+    =
+    (\omega_c\rc)^2
+    =
+    \vchar{}^2.
+    \label{eq:rt_rotor_speed_tautology}
+\end{align}
+\textbf{[DERIVED IDENTITY / NOT A VALIDATION].}
+The equality follows from the definitions of $K_\ell$ and
+$\mathcal K_\omega$ and cannot be cited as an independent internal check of
+the rotor model.
+
+\paragraph{Twist/bend channel guard.}
+A local nearest-neighbour rotor chain supports, in its long-wavelength limit,
+a linear internal twist or phase branch,
+\begin{align}
+    \omega_{\rm twist}(k)
+    =
+    c_{\rm twist}|k|+O(k^3).
+\end{align}
+A slender vortex centreline instead carries transverse Kelvin bending modes
+with the asymptotic structure
+\begin{align}
+    \omega_{\rm K}(k)
+    \simeq
+    \frac{\Gamma_0k^2}{4\pi}
+    \left[
+        \ln\!\left(\frac{1}{|k|a_{\rm core}}\right)
+        +C_{\rm core}
+    \right]
+    \label{eq:rt_kelvin_bending_dispersion_guard}
+\end{align}
+for a specified finite-core profile \cite{Saffman1992}.
+\textbf{[CRITICAL NOTE].}
+The compact rotor may model an internal twist/phase degree of freedom only.
+It must not be identified with transverse filament bending without an explicit
+coupling that reproduces Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_kelvin_bending_dispersion_guard}.
+
+\paragraph{Participation-gap diagnostic.}
+Converting the rotor microinertia to a translational inertia with the
+microscopic length $\rc$ would give
+\begin{align}
+    \rho_{\rm eff}^{(\rc)}
+    :=
+    \frac{\mathcal J_{\omega}^{\rm rot}}{\rc^2}
+    =
+    \pi\rhohorn
+    =
+    1.2231589\times10^{19}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}},
+    \label{eq:rt_rc_scale_effective_inertia}
+\end{align}
+not the calibrated $\rhoF$.  The required scalar participation ratio is
+therefore
+\begin{align}
+    \boxed{
+    \phi_{\rm dyn}
+    :=
+    \frac{\rhoF}{\rho_{\rm eff}^{(\rc)}}
+    =
+    \frac{\rhoF}{\pi\rhohorn}
+    =
+    5.7229\times10^{-26}
+    }.
+    \label{eq:rt_participation_gap}
+\end{align}
+The same missing normalization can be represented by the equivalent length
+\begin{align}
+    \boxed{
+    \ell_{\rho,\rm eq}
+    :=
+    \sqrt{\frac{\mathcal J_{\omega}^{\rm rot}}{\rhoF}}
+    =
+    \frac{\rc}{\sqrt{\phi_{\rm dyn}}}
+    =
+    5.8897\,\mathrm{mm}
+    }.
+    \label{eq:rt_equivalent_response_length}
+\end{align}
+\textbf{[DERIVED DIAGNOSTIC / NOT A LENGTH PREDICTION].}
+Equations~\eqref{eq:rt_participation_gap} and
+\eqref{eq:rt_equivalent_response_length} are reversible reparameterizations
+of the calibrated response.  They do not establish a coherence length, cell
+spacing, correlation length, or physical separation between vortex strings.
+
+\paragraph{Weighted macro-response representation.}
+A future homogenization may write the response schematically as
+\begin{align}
+    \boldsymbol\rho_{\rm eff}(\omega,\mathbf k)
+    =
+    \mu_{\ell}^{\rm horn}\,
+    \boldsymbol\nu_M(\omega,\mathbf k),
+    \qquad
+    [\boldsymbol\nu_M]=\mathrm{m^{-2}},
+    \label{eq:rt_weighted_macro_response}
+\end{align}
+where $\boldsymbol\nu_M$ is a weighted response measure generated by the
+resolved network geometry, mode shapes, boundary conditions, and frequency.
+It is not automatically a literal count of parallel strings.  In a passive
+quasi-static branch the corresponding kinetic quadratic form must be
+non-negative; at finite frequency the effective response may be dispersive,
+anisotropic, or resonant.  The Canon scalar is only the isotropic
+long-wavelength limit of Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_weighted_macro_response}, as fixed
+by Eq.~\eqref{eq:rhof_quasistatic_response_limit}.
+
+\paragraph{Promotion gates.}
+A micro--macro derivation of $\rhoF$ may be promoted only if it:
+\begin{enumerate}
+    \item computes the participation functional without using $\rhoF$,
+          $\phi_{\rm dyn}$, or $\ell_{\rho,\rm eq}$ as fitted inputs;
+    \item converges under representative-volume size, discretization, and
+          boundary-condition refinement;
+    \item preserves the twist/bend distinction and the calibrated units; and
+    \item predicts at least one independent response observable in addition
+          to the target value of $\rhoF$.
+\end{enumerate}
+Until these gates close, $\rhoF$ remains
+\textbf{[CALIBRATED QUASI-STATIC EFFECTIVE RESPONSE]}.
+
